  "question": {
    "text": "A galvanometer, whose resistance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>ohm</mi></math> , has <math><mn>25</mn></math> divisions in it. When a current of <math><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></math> A passes through it, its needle (pointer) deflects by one division. To use this galvanometer as a voltmeter of range <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2.5</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> it should be connected to a resistance of:",
